\section{Radiation Effects on Materials}
IEs consist of semiconductor layers (SiO2, SiN, etc.), which absorb radiation differently than pure silicon.
\begin{itemize}
    \item Ionisation losses increase as dose increases (typically \(10^{-4}\)–\(10^{-5}\) rads/cm²).
    \item Annealing reduces sensitivity to radiation-induced defects.
    \item Post-processing treatments (annealing, cleaning) can improve surface quality.
\end{itemize}

\section{Electrode Design for IEs}
For IEs, the key design constraint is: maximise gain while minimising loss.
\begin{itemize}
    \item Select materials with high electron mobility (typically 500–2000cm²/V·s for IEs).
    \item Use metal gates (Al, Au) or metal-oxide contacts (AlGaN, InGaN).
    \item Keep dimensions optimised for space constraints.
\end{itemize}

\section{Conclusion}
Radiation-enhanced assembly (RCA) offers significant advantages for IEs, particularly for heterogeneous integration. By understanding radiation-induced material changes and optimising electrode geometries, devices can achieve higher performance and reliability.
